Q: Split string on spaces so that substrings are not longer than 30 characters and do not break words A: <p>To split a string on spaces after not more than 30 characters, I'd use <code>preg_split()</code> with a pattern which greedily matches 30 or less characters, forgets those characters then splits on the following space. To ensure that the final segment of the string is not prematurely split allow the pattern to match (and ignore) substrings that reach the end of the string without needing another split. <a href="https://3v4l.org/NtdUT#v" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a></p>
<p>I should state that there is a logical risk that all 90 characters cannot be split into 3 30-max character strings without cutting words. How you want to handle that edge case will need to be clarified.</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>$str = &quot;test string more and more text very long string can be upto length of 90&quot;;
var_export(
    preg_split(
        '#.{1,30}\K(?: |$(*SKIP)(*FAIL))#',
        $str
        //, 3 if you want a max of 3 elements
    )
);
</code></pre>
<p>Output:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>array (
  0 =&gt; 'test string more and more text',
  1 =&gt; 'very long string can be upto',
  2 =&gt; 'length of 90',
)
</code></pre>
<p>If you want to ensure 3 elements even for short strings, union an array of default values to the function call. <a href="https://3v4l.org/9DqOB#v" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a></p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>$str = &quot;short string&quot;;
var_export(
    preg_split(
        '#.{1,30}\K(?: |$(*SKIP)(*FAIL))#',
        $str
    ) + ['', '', '']
);
</code></pre>
<p>Output:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>array (
  0 =&gt; 'short string',
  1 =&gt; '',
  2 =&gt; '',
)
</code></pre>

Q: How do i make my calculator to show each buttons value upon click in the display? A: <ol>
<li>The query to get all the buttons of class <code>num</code> is incorrect. To select all the elements by their class, use dot <code>.</code>. You can see more selectors <a href="https://www.w3schools.com/cssref/css_selectors.asp" rel="nofollow noreferrer">here</a></li>
<li>Use innerHTML to set/get their "inner html" value (I wouldn't recommend this approach though) </li>
<li>You can use the same solution for the elements of class <code>op</code></li>
</ol>

<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>let buttons = document.querySelectorAll('.num');
for (let i=0; i&lt; buttons.length; i++){
  let button = buttons[i];
  button.addEventListener('click' , () =&gt; {
    document.getElementById("display").innerHTML += button.innerHTML
  })
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;div id="calculator"&gt;
    &lt;button class="num"&gt;0&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;button class="num"&gt;1&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;button class="num"&gt;2&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;button class="num"&gt;3&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;button class="num"&gt;4&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;button class="num"&gt;5&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;button class="num"&gt;6&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;button class="num"&gt;7&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;button class="num"&gt;8&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;button class="num"&gt;9&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;button class="op"&gt;+&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;button class="op"&gt;-&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;button class="op"&gt;*&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;button class="op"&gt;/&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;button class="op"&gt;=&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;div id="display"&gt;0&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
